if I had to go back and start over,

00:01:28.744 --> 00:01:30.453
I've been doing this show for 10 years,

00:01:30.453 --> 00:01:32.120
but if I had to go back and start over,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.108
YouTube hands down.

00:01:34.108 --> 00:01:35.692
I mean it didn't exist then.

00:01:35.692 --> 00:01:37.847
Even Gary Vee was using like Viddler

00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:39.183
or something back then,

00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:40.642
which pry doesn't exist anymore.

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:42.063
But YouTube is a better format

00:01:42.063 --> 00:01:44.050
so props to you for getting on that.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.365
- Cool. Well before we dive too deep,

00:01:46.365 --> 00:01:48.557
can you just give my audience a little bit

00:01:48.557 --> 00:01:50.224
of an overview of the The Art of Charm,

00:01:50.224 --> 00:01:51.927
what you're all about, what you're doing,

00:01:51.927 --> 00:01:53.065
a little background.

00:01:53.065 --> 00:01:56.112
- Absolutely, so what I
do at The Art of Charm,

00:01:56.112 --> 00:01:58.839
there's a show of course by
the same name which I host,

00:01:58.839 --> 00:02:01.401
but what we are is both
a show and a school.

00:02:01.401 --> 00:02:03.222
The school is located in LA

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:05.496
and people fly in from all over the world

00:02:05.496 --> 00:02:08.061
and learn about things like
non-verbal communication,

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:11.836
body language, persuasion,
influence, networking,

00:02:11.836 --> 00:02:13.461
things like that.

00:02:13.461 --> 00:02:16.142
So a lot of soft skills that we make,

00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:18.054
I like to say we turn
'em into hard skills,

00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:19.883
although that technically makes no sense,

00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:21.623
but what I mean by that is,

00:02:21.623 --> 00:02:24.466
instead of being like just
be there for the person,

00:02:24.466 --> 00:02:26.128
it's like no, no, what does that mean.

00:02:26.128 --> 00:02:27.511
What does it mean to
give emotional support.

00:02:27.511 --> 00:02:28.891
What does it mean to be persuasive.

00:02:28.891 --> 00:02:31.041
It doesn't mean this,
that, and the other thing.

00:02:31.041 --> 00:02:33.923
It's not what you read
on some blog article

00:02:33.923 --> 00:02:36.887
by some buddy who's trying
to do content marketing.

00:02:36.887 --> 00:02:39.528
I'll have a real person in
here talking about this.

00:02:39.528 --> 00:02:42.489
And so, on the show, we try to also teach

00:02:42.489 --> 00:02:44.563
this very similar and same content.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:47.198
So I'll like, I had Tony Hawk on recently

00:02:47.198 --> 00:02:49.025
and he wasn't just talking about skating,

00:02:49.025 --> 00:02:51.581
he was talking about look
man, building a business

00:02:51.581 --> 00:02:54.218
in this world that nobody
cared about for 10 years

00:02:54.218 --> 00:02:57.590
and then became this cut throat
competitive market space.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.070
Like why did he choose to make that brand.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.686
How has he protected his brand.

00:03:01.686 --> 00:03:03.674
How has he become famous in the face

00:03:03.674 --> 00:03:06.637
of all of this corporate
industrial might in his face.

00:03:06.637 --> 00:03:08.506
I had General McChrystal on,

00:03:08.506 --> 00:03:11.471
who was our theater commander
in Afghanistan and Iraq.

00:03:11.471 --> 00:03:14.768
Special forces guy and just,
he was dismissed by Obama.

00:03:14.768 --> 00:03:17.201
So I went through like, how
do you make tough decisions

00:03:17.201 --> 00:03:19.602
where people could die and then also

00:03:19.602 --> 00:03:21.429
on the other end of that if you screw up,

00:03:21.429 --> 00:03:22.728
not only are people going to die,

00:03:22.728 --> 00:03:24.029
but then your career is over.

00:03:24.029 --> 00:03:26.142
And so, I talk about the
decision-making process

00:03:26.142 --> 00:03:27.763
when it comes to that because I think

00:03:27.763 --> 00:03:30.117
that those of us where
our biggest decision

00:03:30.117 --> 00:03:32.473
is do I go to Starbucks or Coffee Bean

00:03:32.473 --> 00:03:36.583
can also benefit from
different modes of thinking.

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:38.533
So I try to convey mindset in the show

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:43.290
and again it's all stuff that
practical and applicable,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.282
not just like you can do it.

00:03:45.282 --> 00:03:47.722
Because I think inspiration
is cheap, you know.

00:03:47.722 --> 00:03:51.131
It's so easy to find and
actually hard to apply

00:03:51.131 --> 00:03:52.349
and become successful.

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:54.825
So those are the people
I like to deconstruct.

00:03:54.825 --> 00:03:55.879
- I love it.

00:03:55.879 --> 00:03:58.515
Now, talking about being
able to talk yourself

00:03:58.515 --> 00:04:00.388
into or out of any situation,

00:04:00.388 --> 00:04:02.378
what are some of the things

00:04:02.378 --> 00:04:04.084
that people need to keep in mind?

00:04:04.084 --> 00:04:06.037
Or when somebody shows up at your school,

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:07.825
kind of day one newbie,

00:04:07.825 --> 00:04:10.988
what are some of the
most foundational things

00:04:10.988 --> 00:04:13.307
that you need to break or
they need to understand

00:04:13.307 --> 00:04:15.050
before they're ready to move forward

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.226
with some of the tactics?

00:04:16.226 --> 00:04:17.240
- Sure, definitely.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.004
This is kind of a funny question in a way.

00:04:20.004 --> 00:04:24.004
Because there are two
sides to the learning coin

00:04:25.327 --> 00:04:27.394
the first day at AOC.

00:04:27.394 --> 00:04:29.058
The first time people come in

00:04:29.058 --> 00:04:32.590
generally our perception
of how good we are at,

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.391
how I should say how well we have a handle

00:04:35.391 --> 00:04:38.073
on these skills is always way off.

00:04:38.073 --> 00:04:40.269
People either come in thinking

00:04:40.269 --> 00:04:42.260
look I'm really good with people,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:43.762
I'm great at generating rapport,

00:04:43.762 --> 00:04:46.003
I'm pretty good at reading body language,

00:04:46.003 --> 00:04:48.355
I'm pretty good at the confidence thing,

00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:50.668
but I got to work on A, B, C, D.

00:04:50.668 --> 00:04:51.925
And I'm like okay great.

00:04:51.925 --> 00:04:53.426
We take that down on the intake form

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:54.890
and we have a conversation about that.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:58.707
Or they come in and this
is more like guys like me,

00:04:58.707 --> 00:05:01.303
come in and go, I'm not that good at this,

00:05:01.303 --> 00:05:03.782
I'm not that good that, I'm
really not good at this,

00:05:03.782 --> 00:05:06.055
and then I have to, as an instructor,

00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:09.221
my team in LA, Johnny and AJ and Fern

00:05:09.221 --> 00:05:10.884
and all those cats down in LA,

00:05:10.884 --> 00:05:13.447
they have to take that guy and go no,

00:05:13.447 --> 00:05:16.091
here's actual proof that
you're better at these things

00:05:16.091 --> 00:05:18.570
than you know and now we're
going to build the skill set.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.639
So one of the things that
we do on the very first day

00:05:20.639 --> 00:05:23.531
is we video tape people
in real interactions

00:05:23.531 --> 00:05:25.889
and then we show them their body language,

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:27.473
their non-verbal communication,

00:05:27.473 --> 00:05:29.542
the way they sit, stand, walk and talk.

00:05:29.542 --> 00:05:31.326
And either they're thrilled,

00:05:31.326 --> 00:05:33.275
because we're building on this foundation

00:05:33.275 --> 00:05:34.656
that they didn't know they had

00:05:34.656 --> 00:05:37.585
or they're ready, I won't say
horrendously disappointed,

00:05:37.585 --> 00:05:39.984
but other people are less than thrilled,

00:05:39.984 --> 00:05:41.688
but they are then ready to learn

00:05:41.688 --> 00:05:44.690
because their ego has
kind of been white washed

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:47.292
by the video tape because
that tape don't lie.

00:05:47.292 --> 00:05:48.508
Does that make sense?

00:05:48.508 --> 00:05:51.271
- Yeah, it does. So nobody has that...

00:05:51.271 --> 00:05:55.438
For me, I think about people's
relationship with money.

00:05:56.793 --> 00:05:58.131
I don't think people grow up

00:05:58.131 --> 00:05:59.918
having a healthy relationship with money.

00:05:59.918 --> 00:06:02.317
They either, like it's everything

00:06:02.317 --> 00:06:04.594
and it's the only thing that matters,

00:06:04.594 --> 00:06:07.720
or like I don't deserve
money, like I shouldn't,

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.260
I can't go out and get that.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:11.777
So at some point you need to
learn the right relationship.

00:06:11.777 --> 00:06:13.241
And so I think it's the same thing.

00:06:13.241 --> 00:06:15.191
People don't have the right
expectations going in.

00:06:15.191 --> 00:06:17.424
That's interesting.

00:06:17.424 --> 00:06:19.899
What are some of the basics then?

00:06:19.899 --> 00:06:21.400
Like I want to be more charming.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.999
I can see how that's
going to impact my career,

00:06:23.999 --> 00:06:25.952
my business, my relationships.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:27.572
What are some of the foundational things

00:06:27.572 --> 00:06:29.035
that you work people through?

00:06:29.035 --> 00:06:30.901
- Sure, so I'd love to
give a practical drill

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:33.179
slash exercise that everybody watching,

00:06:33.179 --> 00:06:35.498
hanging out with us can
actually apply right away

00:06:35.498 --> 00:06:36.962
starting today, is that cool?

00:06:36.962 --> 00:06:38.548
Does that fit your model?

00:06:38.548 --> 00:06:39.725
- Yeah, do it. I love it.

00:06:39.725 --> 00:06:40.784
Can I do something?

00:06:40.784 --> 00:06:42.489
Am I moving around, what am I doing?

00:06:42.489 --> 00:06:43.711
- Yeah, moving around.

00:06:43.711 --> 00:06:44.887
Well okay, so essentially,

00:06:44.887 --> 00:06:46.429
and I'm going to stand up too

00:06:46.429 --> 00:06:48.584
and I know that made no
difference what so ever.

00:06:48.584 --> 00:06:49.802
(laughs)

00:06:49.802 --> 00:06:53.258
When I sit, I'm as tall as
I am when I stand on camera.

00:06:53.258 --> 00:06:54.681
So the way that this works,

00:06:54.681 --> 00:06:56.181
this is called the doorway drill

00:06:56.181 --> 00:06:57.807
and I'm going to back up and talk about

00:06:57.807 --> 00:06:59.552
why this is important at first.

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:02.069
So the doorway drill is essentially

00:07:02.069 --> 00:07:04.152
a first impression drill.

00:07:05.156 --> 00:07:07.590
And most people think
their first impression,

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:09.300
I don't think it's up for debate,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.372
your first impression is
very important, bla bla bla,

00:07:11.372 --> 00:07:13.444
you never get a second chance
to make your first impression.

00:07:13.444 --> 00:07:15.146
Cliche here, cliche there.

00:07:15.146 --> 00:07:17.464
That stuff is well known by everybody,

00:07:17.464 --> 00:07:19.701
but what most people do not know

00:07:19.701 --> 00:07:22.507
is that your first impression is not made

00:07:22.507 --> 00:07:25.148
when you open your mouth to say something.

00:07:25.148 --> 00:07:28.238
It's not made when you want
to make that first impression.

00:07:28.238 --> 00:07:29.988
And that's very important to know.

00:07:29.988 --> 00:07:32.058
What I mean by that, and
just to put this kind

00:07:32.058 --> 00:07:33.965
of like a funny dating context,

00:07:33.965 --> 00:07:36.114
is if you're a guy and you're walking up

00:07:36.114 --> 00:07:37.540
to a group of women,

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.418
your first impression is
not made when you walk up

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:42.289
and you're like hey ladies
what are you drinking

00:07:42.289 --> 00:07:45.411
or whatever you know corny
thing you've got on deck.

00:07:45.411 --> 00:07:46.710
Your first impression is made

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:49.878
when you become a blip
on other people's radar.

00:07:49.878 --> 00:07:52.353
And so, what that means essentially is

00:07:52.353 --> 00:07:56.207
that when they see you
that's your first impression

00:07:56.207 --> 00:07:58.481
and the next 20 minutes of you hanging out

00:07:58.481 --> 00:07:59.902
with your friends and interacting

00:07:59.902 --> 00:08:01.892
with other people and not them,

00:08:01.892 --> 00:08:05.339
that makes up the bulk
of your first impression.

00:08:05.339 --> 00:08:07.168
It's not controlled by you.

00:08:07.168 --> 00:08:08.709
And what that also means,

00:08:08.709 --> 00:08:12.117
since it's not what you say to
them when you want to say it,

00:08:12.117 --> 00:08:14.189
it means that it's based on a visual

00:08:14.189 --> 00:08:15.567
assessment of who you are

00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:18.008
and this goes back to
Evolutionary Psychology.

00:08:18.008 --> 00:08:20.406
And women are much better
at this than men are

00:08:20.406 --> 00:08:22.758
because of the way
that, the safety concern

00:08:22.758 --> 00:08:26.003
that women evolved over
hundreds of thousands of years

00:08:26.003 --> 00:08:27.628
and the human species and stuff

00:08:27.628 --> 00:08:29.496
that doesn't concern us here.

00:08:29.496 --> 00:08:32.133
But what this means essentially for us is

00:08:32.133 --> 00:08:35.099
that the way that you
sit, stand, walk, and talk

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:37.982
is more important than the
words coming out of your mouth,

00:08:37.982 --> 00:08:40.991
especially before you've
actually had a chance

00:08:40.991 --> 00:08:45.182
to come up with your clever
little opener and deliver it.

00:08:45.182 --> 00:08:47.700
And this is extremely,
extremely important to know

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:50.745
because this changes the
time frame, the window

00:08:50.745 --> 00:08:53.024
in which your first impression is made.

00:08:53.024 --> 00:08:54.609
It's much, much earlier.

00:08:54.609 --> 00:08:56.479
So instead of it being when you decide

00:08:56.479 --> 00:08:58.382
to go over there and chat
after you've had three

00:08:58.382 --> 00:09:00.375
Jack and Cokes or whatever you're doing,

00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:03.295
it's made when you walk down
the stairs into the venue

00:09:03.295 --> 00:09:05.935
or it's made when you
step into the board room

00:09:05.935 --> 00:09:06.783
for the meeting.

00:09:06.783 --> 00:09:09.096
It's not made the first
time you say something.

00:09:09.096 --> 00:09:12.392
That's counteracting your first
impression or bolstering it.

00:09:12.392 --> 00:09:14.827
It's not your actual first impression.

00:09:14.827 --> 00:09:16.047
When I worked on Wall Street,

00:09:16.047 --> 00:09:18.809
I used to be an attorney
in a past life here.

00:09:18.809 --> 00:09:20.925
And I used to sit in
meetings and conference calls

00:09:20.925 --> 00:09:22.880
and think of something
really, really smart to say

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.363
and be on my Blackberry and
looking at the case stuff

00:09:25.363 --> 00:09:27.514
and I would say something,
I'd be so proud of myself,

00:09:27.514 --> 00:09:29.260
and then after the meeting inevitably

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.618
one of the partners would
say, "Hey, you know you look

00:09:31.618 --> 00:09:33.321
"a little bit checked out."

00:09:33.321 --> 00:09:35.801
And I'm thinking I'm the one
who spoke in the meeting.

00:09:35.801 --> 00:09:37.142
How am I checked out?

00:09:37.142 --> 00:09:38.849
And I'm checked out
because everything I did

00:09:38.849 --> 00:09:40.634
before I spoke was
looking at my Blackberry,

00:09:40.634 --> 00:09:43.432
looking at the case law,
looking at the facts,

00:09:43.432 --> 00:09:45.830
trying to make myself small
so nobody called on me,

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:47.173
just like I did in school, right.

00:09:47.173 --> 00:09:48.551
I mean this is very familiar.

00:09:48.551 --> 00:09:50.982
So the doorway drill
seeks to counteract that

00:09:50.982 --> 00:09:53.865
by building up your
non-verbal communication,

00:09:53.865 --> 00:09:56.303
which is actually the bulk
of your first impression.

00:09:56.303 --> 00:09:57.970
Because remember, it's
not about what we say,

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:00.527
it's about what we look like beforehand.

00:10:00.527 --> 00:10:02.111
And most of us are so self-absorbed

00:10:02.111 --> 00:10:04.871
we don't manage our
non-verbal communication.

00:10:04.871 --> 00:10:07.633
So the way that we do this
is we stand up straight,

00:10:07.633 --> 00:10:09.667
chin up, chest, shoulders back,

00:10:09.667 --> 00:10:11.213
and a smile on your face, right.

00:10:11.213 --> 00:10:12.558
And you don't have to exaggerate it.

00:10:12.558 --> 00:10:14.995
You don't have to look
like a serious weirdo

00:10:14.995 --> 00:10:16.374
when you're doing it.

00:10:16.374 --> 00:10:19.094
You're supposed to memorize
and sort of get a feeling

00:10:19.094 --> 00:10:20.881
for how your body feels
when you're doing this.

00:10:20.881 --> 00:10:23.361
And this is very important because

00:10:23.361 --> 00:10:25.964
we need to relegate
this the level of habit.

00:10:25.964 --> 00:10:27.549
Because what most people do is they go,

00:10:27.549 --> 00:10:30.312
okay, my first impression's
made not when I open my mouth,

00:10:30.312 --> 00:10:32.385
it's made when I become
a blip on the radar.

00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:34.292
Okay, so next time I go
to this networking event

00:10:34.292 --> 00:10:36.524
or next time I go out, or
next time go get coffee,

00:10:36.524 --> 00:10:38.392
or next time I do this,
I'm going to make sure

00:10:38.392 --> 00:10:40.503
that I stand up straight
and I got my shoulders back

00:10:40.503 --> 00:10:41.978
and my chin's up, but the problem is

00:10:41.978 --> 00:10:45.219
if you try to manage
this stuff consciously

00:10:45.219 --> 00:10:48.141
what happens is now I'm
talking to you and I'm,

00:10:48.141 --> 00:10:49.605
you're saying something and I'm like

00:10:49.605 --> 00:10:50.941
oh is my chin up, are my shoulders back,

00:10:50.941 --> 00:10:52.363
okay, I have a smile on my face.

00:10:52.363 --> 00:10:53.904
Oh crap, what was Evan talking about.

00:10:53.904 --> 00:10:55.125
Oh my god, I wasn't listening.

00:10:55.125 --> 00:10:56.423
He's going to know I wasn't listening.

00:10:56.423 --> 00:10:57.885
Wait, oh crap, he's still talking

00:10:57.885 --> 00:10:59.306
and I'm still, ahhh, negative self talk,

00:10:59.306 --> 00:11:00.525
feedback loop from hell

00:11:00.525 --> 00:11:02.719
and then the plane just
crashing into the mountain.

00:11:02.719 --> 00:11:03.777
(laughs)

00:11:03.777 --> 00:11:06.337
And so, what the doorway drill does is

00:11:06.337 --> 00:11:08.738
if we remember how this
feels and how it looks,

00:11:08.738 --> 00:11:11.378
and we do this every time
we walk through a doorway,

00:11:11.378 --> 00:11:14.544
we reset our physiology
to this particular set

00:11:14.544 --> 00:11:17.995
of this particular stance,
this particular body language,

00:11:17.995 --> 00:11:20.754
this open body language,
positive body language,

00:11:20.754 --> 00:11:22.703
we do this even in our own house.

00:11:22.703 --> 00:11:25.987
When you walk into your
living room, your bathroom,

00:11:25.987 --> 00:11:28.543
your kid's bedroom, your
front door, whatever,

00:11:28.543 --> 00:11:29.887
if you do this all the time,

00:11:29.887 --> 00:11:32.848
you're going to do this dozens
and dozens of time every day.

00:11:32.848 --> 00:11:35.362
And this is going to setup
your physiology differently

00:11:35.362 --> 00:11:39.141
so that you no longer have
to remember to do this,

00:11:39.141 --> 00:11:41.014
because you're building the habit

00:11:41.014 --> 00:11:43.495
of creating this phsyiology in yourself.

00:11:43.495 --> 00:11:46.056
And so what that means
that the next time you do

00:11:46.056 --> 00:11:48.289
go down to Starbucks
or next time you do go,

00:11:48.289 --> 00:11:49.465
or I guess you're in Canada,

00:11:49.465 --> 00:11:53.046
so next time you do go down
to Tim Horton's, right.

00:11:53.046 --> 00:11:54.752
I grew up in Detroit so I got it.

00:11:54.752 --> 00:11:56.699
- We have Starbuck too,
we have Starbucks too.

00:11:56.699 --> 00:11:57.836
- I'm sure you do.

00:11:57.836 --> 00:11:59.676
I prefer Tim Horton's coffee though.

00:11:59.676 --> 00:12:01.466
That stuff is amaze-bomb.

00:12:01.466 --> 00:12:04.714
In Detroit, we basically
were a short hop to Windsor

00:12:04.714 --> 00:12:07.930
to go get everything, including
alcohol when you're 19.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:09.635
So that was my childhood.

00:12:09.635 --> 00:12:12.355
Explains a lot actually.

00:12:12.355 --> 00:12:14.953
But what that means is that
next time you go out the door,

00:12:14.953 --> 00:12:17.028
you don't have to worry or even think

00:12:17.028 --> 00:12:19.219
about your body language
and your physiology,

00:12:19.219 --> 00:12:20.798
your non-verbal communication,

00:12:20.798 --> 00:12:22.789
because you have relegated,

00:12:22.789 --> 00:12:25.106
hopefully at this point a few weeks later

00:12:25.106 --> 00:12:26.809
of doing the doorway drill,

00:12:26.809 --> 00:12:29.209
you've relegated this
to the level of habit

00:12:29.209 --> 00:12:31.607
so that next time you're
interacting with people,

00:12:31.607 --> 00:12:32.823
you're physiology is different.

00:12:32.823 --> 00:12:35.259
Your first impressions
are therefore different.

00:12:35.259 --> 00:12:37.082
And this creates a virtuous cycle

00:12:37.082 --> 00:12:39.356
because when you look a certain way

00:12:39.356 --> 00:12:41.714
and you start to behave a little bit

00:12:41.714 --> 00:12:43.615
with the way of that physiology,

00:12:43.615 --> 00:12:46.133
people start to treat you differently.

00:12:46.133 --> 00:12:48.694
And this is something that
happens subconsciously.

00:12:48.694 --> 00:12:50.317
We're wired this way as humans.

00:12:50.317 --> 00:12:52.678
People treat you like
you're more friendly, open,

00:12:52.678 --> 00:12:55.523
outgoing, confident, whatever
sort of positive attribute

00:12:55.523 --> 00:12:57.474
they attribute to this physiology.

00:12:57.474 --> 00:12:59.429
And when people treat you differently,

00:12:59.429 --> 00:13:02.149
this, as we know from
science and research,

00:13:02.149 --> 00:13:04.988
is the primary factor
that influences behavior.

00:13:04.988 --> 00:13:06.448
How you're treated by your parents

00:13:06.448 --> 00:13:07.871
influences the way you grow up.

00:13:07.871 --> 00:13:09.215
How you're treated by your classmates

00:13:09.215 --> 00:13:10.880
influences the way that you socialize.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:12.583
So if you change your physiology

00:13:12.583 --> 00:13:14.294
and people start treating you differently,

00:13:14.294 --> 00:13:16.610
your behavior starts to change as well.

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:18.116
So it's a virtuous cycle.

00:13:18.116 --> 00:13:20.269
As people are treating you as an open,

00:13:20.269 --> 00:13:21.447
friendly, confident person,

00:13:21.447 --> 00:13:23.521
then you start to behave more that way,

00:13:23.521 --> 00:13:25.833
which also reinforces your new physiology,

00:13:25.833 --> 00:13:28.679
which means that at some
level or at some stage

00:13:28.679 --> 00:13:31.849
in the next three, four weeks
and few months after that,

00:13:31.849 --> 00:13:34.250
you're going to experience over time,

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:37.866
essentially an identity level
shift, a core level shift

00:13:37.866 --> 00:13:40.437
in who you think that you are

00:13:40.437 --> 00:13:42.428
and who you actually are.

00:13:42.428 --> 00:13:44.783
Instead of being the quiet, meek person,

00:13:44.783 --> 00:13:47.262
or instead of being somebody
who's maybe afraid to speak up,

00:13:47.262 --> 00:13:49.696
or somebody who feels ill
at ease in their own skin

00:13:49.696 --> 00:13:51.688
in networking events,
or in business meetings,

00:13:51.688 --> 00:13:53.316
or in a dating scenario,

00:13:53.316 --> 00:13:54.985
you start to be treated differently.

00:13:54.985 --> 00:13:56.000
You start to act differently.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:57.668
You start to be differently.

00:13:57.668 --> 00:13:59.876
And this isn't meta physical baloney.

00:13:59.876 --> 00:14:02.952
This is science-back phsyiology research,

00:14:02.952 --> 00:14:06.163
sort of Amy Cuddy mixed with Art of Charm,

00:14:06.163 --> 00:14:08.680
Tony Robinsish type stuff in here,

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:11.032
science-based is what
we like to stick with.

00:14:11.032 --> 00:14:13.227
And so, the doorway drill is very powerful

00:14:13.227 --> 00:14:15.876
because it requires
basically no commitment

00:14:15.876 --> 00:14:20.103
other than a millisecond
shift in your physiology

00:14:20.103 --> 00:14:21.972
every time you walk through a doorway.

00:14:21.972 --> 00:14:24.291
And it will change the way
that people deal with you

00:14:24.291 --> 00:14:26.405
and it will change the way
that you think about yourself.

00:14:26.405 --> 00:14:29.051
And it changes your abilities
in social situations.

00:14:29.051 --> 00:14:31.527
And so that type of
thing is a really, really

00:14:31.527 --> 00:14:33.849
high leverage exercise that people

00:14:33.849 --> 00:14:36.856
can start doing right way
and see results right away

00:14:36.856 --> 00:14:38.967
without like buying something

00:14:38.967 --> 00:14:42.218
or figuring out how to
create this massive change

00:14:42.218 --> 00:14:44.699
in themselves manually which is very hard

00:14:44.699 --> 00:14:46.894
and basically impossible.

00:14:46.894 --> 00:14:47.727
- I love it.

00:14:47.727 --> 00:14:49.253
So as I'm walking through a door

00:14:49.253 --> 00:14:51.691
when I leave this I'm going
to walk through a door.

00:14:51.691 --> 00:14:53.841
What are the three to five to seven things

00:14:53.841 --> 00:14:55.828
that I need to do with my physiology

00:14:55.828 --> 00:14:57.780
to now have that confidence?

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:59.529
- So the recap is essentially

00:14:59.529 --> 00:15:01.480
that stand up straight, chest up,

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.758
shoulders back, chin
up, smile on your face.

00:15:03.758 --> 00:15:05.308
And it's harder to do when talking

00:15:05.308 --> 00:15:07.218
because it does look
more ridiculous, right.

00:15:07.218 --> 00:15:10.105
It looks like a very fake news presenter.

00:15:10.105 --> 00:15:12.337
But it won't look fake if you simply

00:15:12.337 --> 00:15:14.124
change the way that your physiology works

00:15:14.124 --> 00:15:17.214
because you're not, again
don't exaggerate this too much,

00:15:17.214 --> 00:15:20.340
because you don't want
to feel goofy doing it.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.206
You just want to straighten up.

00:15:22.206 --> 00:15:23.951
Don't over think it, right.

00:15:23.951 --> 00:15:27.532
Just get out of hunched
over computer all day mode,

00:15:27.532 --> 00:15:29.518
which is where most of us are,

00:15:29.518 --> 00:15:32.000
and get into the mode where
your eyes are actually up

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.420
and your chest is actually up.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:35.531
And no arms crossed,
that's kind of the rule.

00:15:35.531 --> 00:15:36.747
You'd be surprised.

00:15:36.747 --> 00:15:38.779
Most people don't walk
with their arms crossed,

00:15:38.779 --> 00:15:41.092
but many people sit
with their arms crossed

00:15:41.092 --> 00:15:43.330
and regardless of whether you're just cold

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:45.036
or you're feeling a little nervous,

00:15:45.036 --> 00:15:46.618
it just signals to other people,

00:15:46.618 --> 00:15:48.568
look this person's not
in the mood right now.

00:15:48.568 --> 00:15:50.923
And so we want to encourage
interaction with us.

00:15:50.923 --> 00:15:52.430
We want to encourage eye contact.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:54.581
We want to encourage a positive outlook

00:15:54.581 --> 00:15:57.102
from other people towards us.

00:15:57.102 --> 00:15:59.377
And the way that we do that
is really simple signals

00:15:59.377 --> 00:16:00.962
through physiology, that's really it.

00:16:00.962 --> 00:16:02.466
And it works for men and women.

00:16:02.466 --> 00:16:04.253
It's not about being a tough guy.

00:16:04.253 --> 00:16:06.120
It's just about straightening up.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.379
So you don't have to worry,

00:16:07.379 --> 00:16:09.535
like oh I did my shoulders back,

00:16:09.535 --> 00:16:11.240
but I didn't do my chin up high enough.

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:12.783
Like just don't over think it.

00:16:12.783 --> 00:16:14.655
That's the number one rule.

00:16:14.655 --> 00:16:15.488
- Got it.

00:16:15.488 --> 00:16:17.742
And so, if people are
standing there like this

00:16:17.742 --> 00:16:20.343
and then they're thinking,
oh okay Jordan said

00:16:20.343 --> 00:16:23.597
I can't cross my arms, what
am I supposed to do now?

00:16:23.597 --> 00:16:24.430
- Right.

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:26.929
- I can see being self
conscious, oh should I...

00:16:26.929 --> 00:16:28.800
- Yes, that's a great question.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:31.522
It's probably the most common question is,

00:16:31.522 --> 00:16:33.835
oh shoot, I always stand
there with my arms crossed.

00:16:33.835 --> 00:16:35.340
What do I do with my hands now?

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:38.301
And so, the answer is whatever you want.

00:16:38.301 --> 00:16:40.497
However, that's a terrible
answer for somebody

00:16:40.497 --> 00:16:42.524
who's an engineer brain for example,

00:16:42.524 --> 00:16:45.568
because they're like, ah
no, whatever I want, no.

00:16:45.568 --> 00:16:47.233
What's the right answer to this question.

00:16:47.233 --> 00:16:48.126
I was a good student.

00:16:48.126 --> 00:16:49.952
I need the right answer to this question.

00:16:49.952 --> 00:16:53.239
And so, I would say either
if you're holding something

00:16:53.239 --> 00:16:55.838
in one hand then fine,
just have the other hand

00:16:55.838 --> 00:16:57.826
in your pocket with your thumb out,

00:16:57.826 --> 00:17:00.265
that way you're not tempted
to do something weird

00:17:00.265 --> 00:17:01.845
in your pocket that looks awkward,

00:17:01.845 --> 00:17:03.390
just you know fingers in, thumb out.

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.950
Or you can just have your hands resting

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:07.620
like this or like this.

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:09.410
You know just clamped and just have them

00:17:09.410 --> 00:17:10.468
in front of yourself.

00:17:10.468 --> 00:17:11.729
It's still a little bit closed.

00:17:11.729 --> 00:17:15.306
It's not super ideal, but it's
certainly better than this.

00:17:15.306 --> 00:17:18.356
What I'm trying to stay away from here is

00:17:18.356 --> 00:17:21.894
making people self conscious
about what they're doing

00:17:21.894 --> 00:17:23.724
even now that they're straightened up.

00:17:23.724 --> 00:17:27.178
Because the truth is it's
just as easy to look awkwark

00:17:27.178 --> 00:17:30.594
by doing something like
this while being straight up

00:17:30.594 --> 00:17:32.221
and then just trying to
look like a tough guy

00:17:32.221 --> 00:17:34.737
or feeling that you're
being perceived that way.

00:17:34.737 --> 00:17:38.065
So whatever you do with
your hands is probably fine.

00:17:38.065 --> 00:17:41.151
It's just a matter of having that open

00:17:41.151 --> 00:17:44.162
and positive body language
and not being as fidgety.

00:17:44.162 --> 00:17:46.156
So yeah, if you're used to doing this

00:17:46.156 --> 00:17:49.330
and then you find yourself
doing a lot of this stuff

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:51.810
all the time because
you're not sure what to do,

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:54.489
the easiest thing that
we teach the AOC students

00:17:54.489 --> 00:17:56.886
at workshops who really
can't stop fidgeting

00:17:56.886 --> 00:17:59.361
is just fingers in pockets but thumbs out.

00:17:59.361 --> 00:18:01.922
That way it's really hard to fidget.

00:18:01.922 --> 00:18:04.561
It's really also hard to do that thing

00:18:04.561 --> 00:18:06.306
where you have your hands in your pockets

00:18:06.306 --> 00:18:08.660
and you just look like
you're really suspicious,

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.897
because you're hiding something.

00:18:10.897 --> 00:18:14.716
Ideally, if you're just hanging
out at a networking event

00:18:14.716 --> 00:18:16.587
or a business meeting
or something like that

00:18:16.587 --> 00:18:18.253
and you have coffee or a beverage,

00:18:18.253 --> 00:18:20.448
you hold that in one hand and other hand

00:18:20.448 --> 00:18:22.925
can either go in your pocket
or just rest on a table.

00:18:22.925 --> 00:18:25.532
It's like I know that this
sounds super, super simple,

00:18:25.532 --> 00:18:28.703
but if you start to look at
what we do on a daily basis,

00:18:28.703 --> 00:18:31.389
most of us, we're just all over the place.

00:18:31.389 --> 00:18:33.304
We're not thinking about it

00:18:33.304 --> 00:18:37.329
and we tend to wear our
emotional state on our sleeves

00:18:37.329 --> 00:18:40.906
non-verbally which is the
natural state of us as humans.

00:18:40.906 --> 00:18:43.345
However, if we look
uncomfortable or unfriendly

00:18:43.345 --> 00:18:46.070
or scared when we do that,
it's not doing us any favors.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:48.310
And it's making that first impression

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:50.787
a pretty poor first impression.

00:18:50.787 --> 00:18:52.614
- Let me ask you this,
this is interesting.

00:18:52.614 --> 00:18:54.927
Cause I will fidget a lot with my ring,

00:18:54.927 --> 00:18:57.244
I know we're both married, I
don't know if you fidget a lot,

00:18:57.244 --> 00:18:58.991
but I'll play with it as I'm thinking,

00:18:58.991 --> 00:19:01.426
like I like to have
something while I'm thinking

00:19:01.426 --> 00:19:03.659
or being creative, not of a nervous

00:19:03.659 --> 00:19:05.811
like I'm afraid to meet this person

00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:07.358
and I'm like what do I do,

00:19:07.358 --> 00:19:09.673
but if I'm in a brainstorming meeting

00:19:09.673 --> 00:19:11.537
or having a conversation with somebody,

00:19:11.537 --> 00:19:15.351
like the ring is coming
off and flicking it around.

00:19:15.351 --> 00:19:17.302
Like I have it on my thumb a lot.

00:19:17.302 --> 00:19:20.517
What do you think about that?

00:19:20.517 --> 00:19:22.873
What's the message I'm
giving out by doing that?

00:19:22.873 --> 00:19:25.794
- I actually haven't seen it
happen that much in real time,

00:19:25.794 --> 00:19:28.319
but what that would
signal to me is actually,

00:19:28.319 --> 00:19:31.481
and what I think your brain is
probably doing subconsciously

00:19:31.481 --> 00:19:34.651
is activating what's called
the default mode network.

00:19:34.651 --> 00:19:36.436
And tell me if this is true for you.

00:19:36.436 --> 00:19:37.695
When you're in the shower,

00:19:37.695 --> 00:19:40.254
are you ever like oh my
god I'm so doing that,

00:19:40.254 --> 00:19:41.560
that is such a good idea.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:43.956
When you're in the shower, driving,

00:19:43.956 --> 00:19:46.719
that's generally kind of
or maybe out for a walk,

00:19:46.719 --> 00:19:50.050
those are kind of the
top three idea generating

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:52.046
things throughout history.

00:19:52.046 --> 00:19:53.914
And if you look at famous people,

00:19:53.914 --> 00:19:55.666
I mean like famous inventors,

00:19:55.666 --> 00:19:59.077
Edison, Einstein, Thoreau, all these guys,

00:19:59.077 --> 00:20:01.194
there's a chart somewhere on the internet

00:20:01.194 --> 00:20:02.458
and I can try to find it

00:20:02.458 --> 00:20:04.448
or maybe one of us can try to find it.

00:20:04.448 --> 00:20:08.351
It charts what their days
were like, from their journals

00:20:08.351 --> 00:20:11.074
which of course all of these
guys left back in the day.

00:20:11.074 --> 00:20:13.067
It charts what their days were like.

00:20:13.067 --> 00:20:16.360
And so every day, a lot of
these guys, Tesla, Edison,

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:18.110
they all took long walks.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:21.320
Anywhere from half an
hour to three plus hours

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:24.611
on any given day and
that was a mandatory part

00:20:24.611 --> 00:20:26.646
of their routine and the reason was,

00:20:26.646 --> 00:20:29.820
in retrospect of course,
they're probably activating

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:31.443
their default mode network.

00:20:31.443 --> 00:20:33.438
Which is their body is doing something

00:20:33.438 --> 00:20:35.426
that's taking up a certain percentage

00:20:35.426 --> 00:20:37.742
of their cognitive resources
in order to manage.

00:20:37.742 --> 00:20:39.411
You're trying not to drop your ring.

00:20:39.411 --> 00:20:40.996
You want to know where it is.

00:20:40.996 --> 00:20:42.741
You're experiencing a certain feeling

00:20:42.741 --> 00:20:43.963
of having it on your thumb.

00:20:43.963 --> 00:20:45.715
Another feeling of having
it on your ring finger.

00:20:45.715 --> 00:20:47.666
Another feeling of
having it on your pinky.

00:20:47.666 --> 00:20:50.675
That is keeping the
logical part of your brain,

00:20:50.675 --> 00:20:52.706
the prefrontal cortex essentially,

00:20:52.706 --> 00:20:55.230
busy enough so that
other parts of your brain

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:57.261
which having hundreds slash thousands

00:20:57.261 --> 00:20:59.169
of times more processing power,

00:20:59.169 --> 00:21:00.755
those things can then fire off

00:21:00.755 --> 00:21:04.124
without being corralled
by the parts of your brain

00:21:04.124 --> 00:21:07.049
that want to make sure that
the ring's on the right finger,

00:21:07.049 --> 00:21:08.633
make sure you don't forget your coffee,

00:21:08.633 --> 00:21:10.420
make sure you pick up
the kids from school.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:12.981
That stuff is all being taken up.

00:21:12.981 --> 00:21:15.380
All that processing
power is being taken up.

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.046
And that's why your brain can

00:21:17.046 --> 00:21:18.388
then come up with great ideas.

00:21:18.388 --> 00:21:20.217
That's why we come up with amazing ideas

00:21:20.217 --> 00:21:22.903
when driving and then
forget how we got home.

00:21:22.903 --> 00:21:25.260
And then we come up with
amazing ideas in the shower

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:27.170
and we think did I wash my hair already.

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:30.627
I mean it depends, pretty soon
I won't have that problem,

00:21:30.627 --> 00:21:32.983
but it will be something
else right at that point.

00:21:32.983 --> 00:21:35.422
And so we're activating
that default mode network.

00:21:35.422 --> 00:21:37.249
It depends on how you're fidgeting.

00:21:37.249 --> 00:21:39.038
If you're fidgeting and
you're looking around

00:21:39.038 --> 00:21:40.701
and you're micromanaging in your head

00:21:40.701 --> 00:21:42.524
how you're being perceived by others,

00:21:42.524 --> 00:21:44.394
that's lack of comfort in your own skin.

00:21:44.394 --> 00:21:45.941
That's going to put people off.

00:21:45.941 --> 00:21:48.298
However, if you're just
messing with your ring

00:21:48.298 --> 00:21:49.926
while you're thinking about something,

00:21:49.926 --> 00:21:51.391
that's very, very natural

00:21:51.391 --> 00:21:53.825
and it's actually probably
helpful for your brain

00:21:53.825 --> 00:21:56.701
to be creative while you're doing that.

00:21:56.701 --> 00:21:57.534
- Right.

00:21:57.534 --> 00:21:59.013
Yeah, I think this whole time I've been...

00:21:59.013 --> 00:22:00.520
(laughs)

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:03.410
Just been playing with the ring.

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:06.126
I think it definitely helps me.

00:22:06.126 --> 00:22:08.966
I'm not thinking of how people judge me.

00:22:08.966 --> 00:22:11.038
I'm thinking, now that you bring it up,

00:22:11.038 --> 00:22:13.329
I just probably won't change it anyway,

00:22:13.329 --> 00:22:15.337
because I got to be me

00:22:15.337 --> 00:22:18.791
but I'm wondering what
the perception would be.

00:22:18.791 --> 00:22:20.131
- Well, if you're not worrying

00:22:20.131 --> 00:22:23.096
about other people judging you doing this

00:22:23.096 --> 00:22:25.125
and you're fidgeting with your ring

00:22:25.125 --> 00:22:26.670
in order to come up with creative ideas,

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:28.130
I'm saying don't change it.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:28.963
- Yeah.

00:22:28.963 --> 00:22:31.789
- If you're one of those
people who like me,

00:22:31.789 --> 00:22:34.270
I just constantly sip
tea while I do the show.

00:22:34.270 --> 00:22:37.237
And I'll sip tea and
then I'll put it down.

00:22:37.237 --> 00:22:39.227
And then two second
later, I'll sip more tea.

00:22:39.227 --> 00:22:42.074
It's almost like if you saw me doing this

00:22:42.074 --> 00:22:44.102
while I was not doing my show,

00:22:44.102 --> 00:22:46.250
you might be like this guy is so weird.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.714
Every time he puts his tea down,

00:22:47.714 --> 00:22:49.420
but I don't worry about that.

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:51.493
I'm not even thinking
about drinking the tea.

00:22:51.493 --> 00:22:54.298
Usually, I do when I'm listening
and not talking of course.

00:22:54.298 --> 00:22:55.922
So if the guest is talking,

00:22:55.922 --> 00:22:58.398
I'm sipping tea, put it
down, sip tea, put it down,

00:22:58.398 --> 00:23:01.483
but I'm processing what the
guest is saying the whole time.

00:23:01.483 --> 00:23:03.272
And that's been very important to me.

00:23:03.272 --> 00:23:04.939
When I don't drink the tea,

00:23:04.939 --> 00:23:06.893
when I don't create something

00:23:06.893 --> 00:23:11.839
from my prefrontal cortex to
do while the guest is talking,

00:23:11.839 --> 00:23:14.157
I've found over the
years that I end up going

00:23:14.157 --> 00:23:15.944
oh man, I'm almost out of cough drops,

00:23:15.944 --> 00:23:17.772
damn it, what was he just talking about,

00:23:17.772 --> 00:23:19.683
I forgot, ah dang, right.

00:23:19.683 --> 00:23:23.458
I just end up with this
weird, kind of hyper brain

00:23:23.458 --> 00:23:26.749
that is not conducive to
being a good listener.

00:23:26.749 --> 00:23:28.658
So I've learned how to cope with that

00:23:28.658 --> 00:23:32.152
by figuring out how to keep
that part of my brain busy.

00:23:32.152 --> 00:23:34.348
And most creative types have done this.

00:23:34.348 --> 00:23:38.123
That's why you see creatives
doing weird stuff all the time.

00:23:38.123 --> 00:23:40.478
And some of the most
creative people around,

00:23:40.478 --> 00:23:43.605
unfortunately, have issues with substances

00:23:43.605 --> 00:23:47.138
that also limit what that
part of your brain can do.

00:23:47.138 --> 00:23:49.409
Because it's driving them freaking crazy

00:23:49.409 --> 00:23:50.992
and it's keeping their potential at bay.

00:23:50.992 --> 00:23:52.822
Unfortunately, there are side effects

00:23:52.822 --> 00:23:54.896
that go along with
alcohol, and painkillers,

00:23:54.896 --> 00:23:57.375
and things like that, but
the reason they start doing

00:23:57.375 --> 00:23:59.486
that stuff in the first place is largely

00:23:59.486 --> 00:24:01.680
because of that creative process.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:03.634
And you hear creatives say things like,

00:24:03.634 --> 00:24:05.298
"I just don't feel like myself

00:24:05.298 --> 00:24:08.631
"when I'm not able to insert vice here."

00:24:09.601 --> 00:24:13.993
So your ring by comparison
is a very healthy alternative

00:24:13.993 --> 00:24:18.160
to meth, heroin, painkillers,
sleeping pills, alcohol,

00:24:19.728 --> 00:24:22.696
so go ahead and fidget with
the ring as much as you want.

00:24:22.696 --> 00:24:25.786
- All right, back to the doorway test.

00:24:25.786 --> 00:24:29.363
I want to understand the
connection with the mind

00:24:29.363 --> 00:24:31.356
and I get that the physiology leads

00:24:31.356 --> 00:24:32.941
to the confidence in the mind.

00:24:32.941 --> 00:24:35.056
I'm wondering is that the only connection

00:24:35.056 --> 00:24:37.937
or is there also, are
you thinking something

00:24:37.937 --> 00:24:39.238
as you walk through the door

00:24:39.238 --> 00:24:41.230
or is it like okay, I'm happy.

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:43.015
Are you telling yourself anything

00:24:43.015 --> 00:24:46.217
or is it just I'm going
to do the body thing

00:24:46.217 --> 00:24:49.677
and the body is going to tell
my mind to do it that way?

00:24:49.677 --> 00:24:51.176
- That's a really good question.

00:24:51.176 --> 00:24:54.509
Actually, one of the maxims here at AOC,

00:24:55.643 --> 00:24:57.345
and again there's science behind this

00:24:57.345 --> 00:24:59.743
that I won't really bore
you all with right now,

00:24:59.743 --> 00:25:00.958
the body follows the mind

00:25:00.958 --> 00:25:02.341
and the mind follows the body.

00:25:02.341 --> 00:25:04.212
And this is like a very, very simple

00:25:04.212 --> 00:25:06.203
kind of almost obvious truth

00:25:06.203 --> 00:25:08.032
that a lot of people don't think about.

00:25:08.032 --> 00:25:11.202
And so, when we do have our
physiology setup correctly,

00:25:11.202 --> 00:25:14.045
we do tend to experience
something in our brain

00:25:14.045 --> 00:25:16.198
that says all right I'm
coming in, I'm feeling good,

00:25:16.198 --> 00:25:17.825
I'm feeling confident, I'm feeling open.

00:25:17.825 --> 00:25:18.925
It's all subconscious.

00:25:18.925 --> 00:25:21.402
It's not something that
you have to tell yourself.

00:25:21.402 --> 00:25:24.646
You shouldn't worry about
like getting yourself

00:25:24.646 --> 00:25:27.409
all jazzed up to go walk into Starbucks.

00:25:27.409 --> 00:25:28.951
It's really not necessary.

00:25:28.951 --> 00:25:31.471
You can if you want to if
you find that it helps,

00:25:31.471 --> 00:25:35.002
Sure, give yourself every time
you walk through the door.

00:25:35.002 --> 00:25:39.169
It might be helpful to sort
of reduce it to one word

00:25:40.654 --> 00:25:44.821
where you just say something
like open or positive

00:25:45.769 --> 00:25:47.722
or whatever or you can just say your name.

00:25:47.722 --> 00:25:49.304
Like you can just be like, Evan.

00:25:49.304 --> 00:25:50.678
Right, I'm in Evan mode now.

00:25:50.678 --> 00:25:52.223
I'm upright, I'm confident, I'm happy,

00:25:52.223 --> 00:25:53.564
I got my smile on my face.

00:25:53.564 --> 00:25:54.397
That's fine.

00:25:54.397 --> 00:25:55.797
It can just be symbolic.

00:25:55.797 --> 00:25:58.559
Our brains are really good with symbolism.

00:25:58.559 --> 00:26:00.752
So if you're going to
create that doorway drill

00:26:00.752 --> 00:26:02.946
and you want to sort of have a mantra word

00:26:02.946 --> 00:26:05.265
that you repeat in your head
when you go through every door

00:26:05.265 --> 00:26:06.279
then go for it.

00:26:06.279 --> 00:26:08.798
I, personally, don't use it that much,

00:26:08.798 --> 00:26:11.075
but a lot of AOC students do use it,

00:26:11.075 --> 00:26:13.063
especially the guys and girls

00:26:13.063 --> 00:26:15.540
that have engineer brains where it's like

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:17.207
they need to flip a switch

00:26:17.207 --> 00:26:19.156
and it can't just be a
subtle physiology thing.

00:26:19.156 --> 00:26:21.794
It's got to be like all
right I'm in this mode now.

00:26:21.794 --> 00:26:23.215
It's got to be something logical

00:26:23.215 --> 00:26:25.288
that takes them into a different mode.

00:26:25.288 --> 00:26:27.763
And if that helps you
then definitely go for it.

00:26:27.763 --> 00:26:31.501
But do avoid the self talk where you go,

00:26:31.501 --> 00:26:33.000
all right I'm walking in here,

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:34.422
I'm going to be upright

00:26:34.422 --> 00:26:36.578
and oh my, there's a lot of people in here

00:26:36.578 --> 00:26:37.958
so I got to make sure I do that

00:26:37.958 --> 00:26:40.762
because that can spiral into what we call

00:26:40.762 --> 00:26:43.969
being in your head which is worrying a lot

00:26:43.969 --> 00:26:46.089
about how you're perceived by others,

00:26:46.089 --> 00:26:47.629
which is the actual opposite

00:26:47.629 --> 00:26:49.738
of what the doorway drill
is supposed to accomplish.

00:26:49.738 --> 00:26:50.714
Does that make sense?

00:26:50.714 --> 00:26:51.646
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:26:51.646 --> 00:26:54.449
People on the live chat, first
of all, they're loving you.

00:26:54.449 --> 00:26:56.323
Lots of comments, I like this guy,

00:26:56.323 --> 00:26:58.393
but also they're laughing because my book

00:26:58.393 --> 00:27:00.017
is called Your One Word.

00:27:00.017 --> 00:27:00.850
- Oh yeah.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:02.093
- So it's like tell yourself one word.

00:27:02.093 --> 00:27:04.002
- That was a little subconscious thing.

00:27:04.002 --> 00:27:05.383
- Nice parallel.

00:27:05.383 --> 00:27:08.550
I think of this documentary's on Regis

00:27:09.811 --> 00:27:13.978
and every time he left his
office to go into studio mode,

00:27:15.339 --> 00:27:18.955
he would also touch the top of the door.

00:27:18.955 --> 00:27:19.788
- Yeah.

00:27:21.071 --> 00:27:23.049
- Do you ever incorporate that?

00:27:23.049 --> 00:27:25.246
Like as your walking through
as a physical reminder

00:27:25.246 --> 00:27:27.845
like touch the side of the
door, touch the top of the door,

00:27:27.845 --> 00:27:29.348
and that sets you up or...

00:27:29.348 --> 00:27:30.566
- You can, you can.

00:27:30.566 --> 00:27:33.700
The reason that I think
he may have done that,

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:36.014
and it's funny I haven't
seen that documentary,

00:27:36.014 --> 00:27:37.519
but I definitely want to check it out.

00:27:37.519 --> 00:27:38.493
He's a funny guy.

00:27:38.493 --> 00:27:40.564
I've seen him a lot cause I used to work

00:27:40.564 --> 00:27:43.528
at Sirius XM radio and he was in there.

00:27:43.528 --> 00:27:46.774
And I remember one time I
was going to the bathroom

00:27:46.774 --> 00:27:50.635
before my show when we were
doing it live in Time Square.

00:27:50.635 --> 00:27:53.037
And I'm going to the bathroom
and I look next to me

00:27:53.037 --> 00:27:55.838
and I see, I look over and
there's this like shorter guy,

00:27:55.838 --> 00:27:57.582
I go oh hi how you doing,

00:27:57.582 --> 00:28:00.062
you know the polite urinal small talk,

00:28:00.062 --> 00:28:01.847
and he goes, "How you doing?"

00:28:01.847 --> 00:28:04.047
And I was just like oh my
god, it's Regis Philbin.

00:28:04.047 --> 00:28:05.754
(laughs)

00:28:05.754 --> 00:28:08.145
Right next to me and man is he tan

00:28:08.145 --> 00:28:11.812
for a guy who lives in
New York, really tan.

00:28:13.101 --> 00:28:14.806
Oh, I lost my train of thought.

00:28:14.806 --> 00:28:16.763
Oh yeah, so tapping the side of the door

00:28:16.763 --> 00:28:18.144
and things like that,

00:28:18.144 --> 00:28:20.823
that will work especially if
it's always the same place.

00:28:20.823 --> 00:28:23.380
I would avoid doing that every time

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:27.113
you walk into the airport,
Starbucks, some random office,

00:28:27.113 --> 00:28:29.874
because it can back fire on you.

00:28:29.874 --> 00:28:33.861
I've definitely seen there
are times when you can't do it

00:28:33.861 --> 00:28:36.133
and you're going to end up
with a physiological hang.

00:28:36.133 --> 00:28:39.510
But for example, this
studio, I walk in here,

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:41.135
every time I walk in here,

00:28:41.135 --> 00:28:43.204
I walk in pretty much the same way.

00:28:43.204 --> 00:28:44.909
I turn on the studio lights.

00:28:44.909 --> 00:28:47.266
I have a remote that turns on
some of these other lights,

00:28:47.266 --> 00:28:49.500
some of which are off that
you can see behind me,

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:51.326
and I've got all my audio gear here

00:28:51.326 --> 00:28:53.519
that has all this digital stuff.

00:28:53.519 --> 00:28:55.352
But I got this analog stuff,

00:28:55.352 --> 00:28:57.913
because it has just a gillion switches.

00:28:57.913 --> 00:28:59.825
And I like flipping those switches

00:28:59.825 --> 00:29:02.952
and watching them warm
up and hearing the zzzzz.

00:29:02.952 --> 00:29:05.270
I like that because that's like all right,

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:07.181
this is happening, we're going on air,

00:29:07.181 --> 00:29:09.818
and I find myself getting a
burst of energy from that.

00:29:09.818 --> 00:29:10.795
It's a ritual.

00:29:10.795 --> 00:29:14.121
Humans are wired for
ritual so I would say yes,

00:29:14.121 --> 00:29:15.138
definitely do it.

00:29:15.138 --> 00:29:17.662
If you get to your office
and the first thing you do

00:29:17.662 --> 00:29:19.734
is make yourself a cup of tea

00:29:19.734 --> 00:29:22.735
and throw in a piece of
Trident or something like that,

00:29:22.735 --> 00:29:24.402
then that can be your ritual.

00:29:24.402 --> 00:29:26.066
But it shouldn't necessary be something

00:29:26.066 --> 00:29:28.344
that you transport with
you every where you go,

00:29:28.344 --> 00:29:29.727
because if you can't do it,

00:29:29.727 --> 00:29:32.656
you end up with an unflipped
switch in your head

00:29:32.656 --> 00:29:35.216
and you'll worry about it
even if subconsciously.

00:29:35.216 --> 00:29:37.980
And also, the other
thing is it loses meaning

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:39.644
if it's something you do every time

00:29:39.644 --> 00:29:41.348
you walk through a doorway.

00:29:41.348 --> 00:29:43.301
Because the point of the doorway drill is

00:29:43.301 --> 00:29:45.087
your physiology is going to stay positive

00:29:45.087 --> 00:29:47.605
and it's going to change the
way that others perceive you.

00:29:47.605 --> 00:29:50.167
But it doesn't have to be
you getting psyched up.

00:29:50.167 --> 00:29:52.076
And so, you shouldn't
need to psych yourself up

00:29:52.076 --> 00:29:53.664
every time you walk through a doorway.

00:29:53.664 --> 00:29:55.822
It would be impossible
to maintain that level

00:29:55.822 --> 00:29:58.265
of energy and emotion throughout the day.

00:29:58.265 --> 00:30:00.175
So the drill would then lose meaning.

00:30:00.175 --> 00:30:02.368
The gesture of tapping the
door would lose meaning.

00:30:02.368 --> 00:30:04.280
You see guys, like you said with Regis,

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:06.797
he taps the doorway to his studio.

00:30:06.797 --> 00:30:09.396
He probably doesn't do that
when he walks into the can.

00:30:09.396 --> 00:30:12.436
Right, he's probably like
this is business as usual.

00:30:12.436 --> 00:30:15.442
The other things on camera
in front of a million people,

00:30:15.442 --> 00:30:18.405
one is decidedly more
important than the other.

00:30:18.405 --> 00:30:19.904
- You got to witness it.

00:30:19.904 --> 00:30:21.532
Did he touch the door, Jordan?

00:30:21.532 --> 00:30:23.405
- I know. Now I'm like
did he touch the door

00:30:23.405 --> 00:30:24.419
before he went in?

00:30:24.419 --> 00:30:25.758
I don't know. I don't know

00:30:25.758 --> 00:30:27.503
if he tapped the side of the door.

00:30:27.503 --> 00:30:28.336
- Cool, man.

00:30:28.336 --> 00:30:31.201
Well, it's amazing how quickly
half an hour flys by, man.

00:30:31.201 --> 00:30:32.093
- Oh my gosh.

00:30:32.093 --> 00:30:33.998
- Really appreciate the love, the energy.

00:30:33.998 --> 00:30:35.666
For people who want to find out more

00:30:35.666 --> 00:30:37.742
about what you're doing,
your school, the podcast,

00:30:37.742 --> 00:30:38.838
where are they going?

00:30:38.838 --> 00:30:39.671
- Sure.

00:30:39.671 --> 00:30:41.312
If people are listening to podcast,

00:30:41.312 --> 00:30:42.816
which I encourage of course,

00:30:42.816 --> 00:30:44.605
you can find The Art of Charm podcast

00:30:44.605 --> 00:30:46.190
in iTunes or any podcast player.

00:30:46.190 --> 00:30:48.341
Or just go to TheArtofCharm.com

00:30:48.341 --> 00:30:49.878
and, of course, it's on SoundCloud

00:30:49.878 --> 00:30:51.137
and YouTube and all that.

00:30:51.137 --> 00:30:52.964
But it is audio only, which is good

00:30:52.964 --> 00:30:54.582
because I want people to absorb it

00:30:54.582 --> 00:30:57.027
and not just be watching my shiny forehead

00:30:57.027 --> 00:30:58.164
here in Studio.

00:30:58.164 --> 00:30:59.951
(laughs)

00:30:59.951 --> 00:31:00.784
- I love it.

00:31:00.784 --> 00:31:02.101
Jordan, you gave so much value.

00:31:02.101 --> 00:31:03.237
Really appreciate it.

00:31:03.237 --> 00:31:05.069
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:31:05.069 --> 00:31:08.652
Continue to believe
and we'll see you soon.

